NAME
    su - change this session's user identity (root only)

SYNOPSIS
    su <uid>[:<gid>]

DESCRIPTION
    Sets the shell's own uid and/or gid (numeric). A builtin, because it changes
    the shell's OWN task identity: a /bin program would only change its
    short-lived child. Commands spawned afterward inherit the new identity, and
    the prompt switches between # (root) and $ (a normal user).

    Forms, like chown:
        uid        set the user, leave the group
        uid:gid    set both
        :gid       set the group, leave the user

    ROOT ONLY. su lets root drop to a user (for testing); a non-root session is
    refused. This is what makes login meaningful: once you log in as a user you
    cannot su back to root - the way back is `logout` and logging in as root.
    (See login. Under the hood the kernel lets a task restore its saved identity,
    but that path is reserved for the shell's own logout; su enforces root-only,
    and a user's child programs can't restore root at all.)

EXAMPLES
    su 1000            (as root) become uid 1000, keep the group
    su 1000:1000       (as root) become uid 1000, gid 1000

SEE ALSO
    login, logout, id, sh
